\section{Reading it}{
 At a heterozygous site the read fractions follow the strain
proportions, so for \code{K} strains the sites fall into bands (Zhu et al. 2019). In the
minor half of that picture:
\itemize{
\item \strong{mass squeezed against zero} -- one dominant strain, with a minor companion or
just sequencing noise. Filtering minor alleles leaves the dominant haplotype intact,
so such a sample can be treated as monoclonal.
\item \strong{a band near 0.5} -- two strains of comparable size. No minor-allele filter can
remove it, because a filter has to stay below 0.5 to keep the dominant call at all;
forcing it would delete a real strain and leave a chimera.
\item \strong{more than one band} -- more than two strains.
}

The dashed line marks \code{comparable_at}: a minor-allele filter has to stay below 0.5 to
keep the dominant call, so mass past that line is what no filter can remove.

Judge the panels by how much \emph{area} sits past the line, not by whether anything does.
A handful of sites near 0.5 in an otherwise empty panel is a few repetitive or
mismapped loci; a second strain shows up across the genome. That is why \code{wsaf_profile}
decides on rates over covered sites, and reports \code{min_freq_needed} -- the filter that
clears a given sample -- rather than a verdict alone.
}

